1 NAME Eskil Isaksson /Banér/
2 GIVN Eskil Isaksson
2 _MARNM Banér
2 NICK Banér
1 NAME Eskeli Iisakinpoika
2 GIVN Eskeli Iisakinpoika
2 SURN
2 NICK Banér
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1415
2 PLAC Röklinge
2 ADDR
3 CITY Faringe
3 STAE Uppland
3 CTRY Sweden
1 DEAT
2 DATE BET 1481 AND 27 AUG 1488
2 ADDR
3 CTRY Sweden
1 OCCU Hövitsman, väpnare, riddare, häadshövding, hövitsman, riksråd
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Eskil Isaksson Banér==
2 CONT
2 CONT Eskil Isaksson, (son of Isak Isaksson, tab 5), to Röklinge in Faringe parish and Venngarn in St. Olof's parish (both in Stockholm County) and Händelö in St. Johannis parish, Östergötland county. Was Army's 1438 and Knights 1441. H
2 CONC erd Chief of the Olands District of Uppland in 1441 and 1466. Hövidsman at Stäkeholm 1445. Was national council 1445 and 1476. Hövidsman Raseborg Was the ruler of Östkind's district (Ög.) 1470. 05-15. Was dead 1488-08-27, when the
2 CONC  succession was held after him. He proved the men's meeting in Telge in 1438. Immediately at the Archbishop Jöns Bengtsson's uprising in 1457, he took his party and participated with this in the regulation of the feasts at the beg
2 CONC inning of 1465. Later on Sten Sture's side stood against King Christian.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Wives==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Married 1: o before 1441-07-08 with Cecilia Haraldsdotter, who got Venngarn in the morning gift and still lived in 1465. She and her husband confirmed 1461-01-25 her mother's gift to Vadstena monastery. Daughter of the knight an
2 CONC d the national council Harald Stensson Gren to Ål (now Grensholmen) in Östergötland and Sigrid Tomasdotter van Vitzen. 
2 CONT * Married 2: o with Brita Arentsdotter, daughter of knight Arent Bengtsson (backward wolf) to Nynäs and Ekolsund and his 2nd wife Hebbla Albrektsdotter Bydelsbach
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Children with Cecilia Haraldsdotter==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Sigrid Eskilsdotter, to Lindholmen in Orkesta parish, Stockholm County. Dead autumn 1527, probably at Gäddeholm (now Tureholm) in Trosa, Södermanland County is buried and buried in Trosa church. In 1520 she was imprisoned by Kin
2 CONC g Christian Tyrann to Copenhagen, where she was held until the beginning of 1524. Was through her first married grandmother to King Gustaf I and through her second married mother to Christina Gyllenstierna. Married 1: o 1475-01-15
2 CONC  with knight and councilor Magnus Karlsson, to Eka, dead 1484. Married 2: o 1487-10-00 with knight and council Nils Eriksson (Gyllenstierna), to Fogelvik, dead 1495.
2 CONT * Knut Eskilsson. Riksråd. Dead about 1518. See Tab. 7
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Children with Brita Arentsdotter==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Nils Eskilsson. Riksråd. Executed 1520. See Tab. 8
2 CONT * Brita Eskilsdotter, to Händelö.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source - http://www.adelsvapen.com/genealogi/Ban%C3%A9r_nr_22#TAB_6
2 CONT
2 CONT Eskil Isaksson (Banér) var ett svenskt riksråd, riddare och herre till Venngarn i Sigtuna, och nämnd mellan 1438 och 1481. Han var häradshövding i Olands härad och hövitsman på flera slott. Han gifte sig första gången 5 juli 1441 
2 CONC med Cecilia Haraldsdotter (Gren) och andra gången med Brita Arentsdotter (Ulf). Herr Eskil levde ännu 1481, men var död 1488, arvsskiftet förrättades 27 augusti 1488.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eskil_Isaksson_Ban%C3%A9r (in Swedish)
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Notes==
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes Ingar
2 CONT
2 CONT This in our history freed, is actually Danish and counting as his ancestor a Tymo or Tymme from Seland, which in 1020 by King Knut shall have been assigned the name Banér for his bravery in a field battle against the English. The 
2 CONC first historically peculiar with this name in Sweden was Eskil Isaksson, who around 1440 is listed among the councils of the kingdom. He left two sons, Knut and Nils (to Djursholm). The latter's grandchildren adopted the family na
2 CONC me Banner (initially Banner and Banner).
2 CONT
2 CONT ==History==
2 CONT
2 CONT Eskil Isaksson (Banér) was a Swedish national councilor, knight, ruler and master of Venngarn, born about 1415 and mentioned between 1438 and 1481. He was the ruler of the county of Olands and the chief of several castles. Eskil I
2 CONC saksson was the son of Isaac Isaksson (Banner), (dead after 1425). He first married on July 5, 1441 with Cecilia Haraldsdotter (Gren) and the second time with Brita Arentsdotter (Ulf). Mr Eskil lived on May 26, 1481, but was dead 
2 CONC when his inheritance was changed on August 27, 1488.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Eskil Isaksson is first mentioned in 1438 in connection with Tälje's meeting, where he vowed allegiance to Karl Knutsson (Bonde), and belonged to the group within the provincial nobility who came to gain advantages during his rule
2 CONC . Through his marriage with Cecilia Haraldsdotter (Gren), he came into possession of significant earthly goods in Östergötland, and came to be attached to the mighty Border family. His wife was the niece of Magnus Gren who was in 
2 CONC favor of Kristofer of Bavaria and this also made Eskil Isaksson involved in the favor. Not later than 1445 he became a national councilor, and a chieftain at Stegeholm. After Karl Knutsson's throne climb, Eskil Isaksson participat
2 CONC ed with Magnus Gren in the expedition to Gotland 1448-49 and on July 31, 1449 signed the surrender when the island was handed over to Kristian I. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT True to Magnus Gren, he signed the 1457 rise against Karl Knutsson and praised Kristian I, and followed Erik Axelsson (Tott) to Finland to receive the homeland's tribute on behalf of King Kristians. He was appointed captain of the
2 CONC  Raseborg castle, a position he held until 1463. During this time he began to turn politically, and abdicated himself from Magnus Gren and Oxenstiernorna and approached the Axels Islands instead. In the battle between Bengt Jönsso
2 CONC n and Erik Axelsson in 1467 he placed himself on the latter's side, and then Karl Knutsson 1470 died and
2 CONT
2 CONT Kristian I tried to regain the royal power in Sweden with the support of Magnus Grens son Ivar Gren, they tried in vain to win Eskil Isaksson's support. At the 1473, 1474, and 1476 Union meetings, he was Swedish delegated, but liv
2 CONC ed in his last years mostly withdrawn on Venngarn, which he inherited in 1452 from a distant relative.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eskil Isaksson was never the lawmate but as his father, the governor of Olands, here in 1441 and 1466, as well as the 1470 ruler of Östkind's quarters.
2 CONT
