1 OCCU Häradshövding i Österrekarne
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Sten Haraldsson (Gren)==
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Sten Haraldsson''', häradshövding i Österrekarne, (levde ännu 1417) på Ål (nu Grensholmen) i Vånga (Ög), blev i äktenskap med en dtr till konung Erik Magnussons råd Nils Magnusson (Ivar Nilssons ätt) far till riksrådet Magnus
2 CONC  Gren.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Married==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Married: [https://www.geni.com/photo/view/297898016850003658?album_type=photos_of_me&photo_id=6000000085005589823 '''Cecilia Nilsdotter,'''] daughter of Nils Magnusson & Kristina Ivarsdotter 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Children ==
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Magnus Gren,''' married Ingeborg Karlsdotter Sture & Gertrud Narvesdotter Rømer.  Magnus Gren, who was the son of the previous owner and brother-in-law with King Charles VIII. It "was he who changed the estate's old name to G
2 CONC rensholm. After his death in the 1470s, the property for a task to have belonged to his son, the Privy Council Ivar and his son, the Privy Council Magnus Branch († 1520), but after another task went to the property in inheritance 
2 CONC Magnus Grens daughter Filippa, married to the Swedish National Council and the knight Bengt Ryning, and then their son the National Council and the gunman Erik Ryning (beheaded 1520). 
2 CONT * '''Erik Stensson (Gren),'''
2 CONT * '''Harald Stensson (Gren),''' married Sigrid Tomasdotter Vitzen
2 CONT
2 CONT Source - https://www.geni.com/photo/view/297898016850003658?album_type=photos_of_me&photo_id=6000000085004124400
2 CONT
2 CONT == SDHK No: [https://sok.riksarkivet.se/?Sokord=Sten+Haraldsson+Gren&EndastDigitaliserat=false&AvanceradSok=False&page=1&postid=sdhk_18457&tab=post&FacettState=undefined%3Ac%7C#tab 18457]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Swedish Diplomatarium's main directory of medieval letters'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Dating: 1415 without a day,
2 CONT
2 CONT Sten Haraldsson (Branch) of Ål and his wife Cecilia Nilsdotter give her estate Vållingstad in Husaby (now Östra Husby) parish (Östkinds herad) to Linköping Cathedral. - https://www.geni.com/photo/view?album_type=photos_of_me&id=29
2 CONC 7898016850003658&photo_id=6000000139919200838&position=0
2 CONT
2 CONT ==History==
2 CONT
2 CONT Grensholm. 
2 CONT
2 CONT also added to Gripsholm's castle, which may have happened only by some confusion of names, such as one's own room, due to the fact that both goods must have been held by the same family for long periods . This is so much more like
2 CONC ly that Gripsholm's castle in the 1370s was built by Bo Jonsson Grip on the farm 
2 CONT Näsby's estates. In ancient times, the gentleman's seat, the old family Green or Gren, belonged to a family that sought to lead its ancestry on the sword side of the Stenkilska royal house. Ar 1280 made a Harald Thorsson to Aal, a
2 CONC nd in 1307 the national council and the knight Sten Haraldsson (Gren).  Aal then belonged to the mighty, but later against his fatherland the faithless national council and the gunman Magnus Gren, who was the son of the previous o
2 CONC wner and brother-in-law with King Charles VIII. It "was he who changed the estate's old name to Grensholm. After his death in the 1470s, the property for a task to have belonged to his son, the Privy Council Ivar and his son, the 
2 CONC Privy Council Magnus Branch († 1520), but after another task went to the property in inheritance Magnus Grens daughter Filippa, married to the Swedish National Council and the knight Bengt Ryning, and then their son the National C
2 CONC ouncil and the gunman Erik Ryning (beheaded 1520). Inherited by his daughter Märta († 1564), who was first married to Äbraham Pedersson Rommel to Wi and Grensholm and then to the National Council and the governor of eastern Gothen
2 CONC burg Jakob Turesson Rosengren.  At this time or in September 1566, Grensholmen had a day's visit by King Erik XIV. After the death of Jakob Rosengren in 1571, the property was attacked by his son, governor Ture  Rosengren († 1611)
2 CONC . With their daughter Metta Rosengren († 1638) the property passed through the marriage to the governor Sven Ribbing († 1640) and was then inherited by the son, the governor Ture Ribbing († restored in 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1656), and further as an old well-built manor without a count of his son, v. the governor Sven Ribbing, who on April 2, 1706, sold the property to the king, the council and the lieutenant general. the president, Count Jakob Spens 
2 CONC (d. 1721) and his Countess Beata Bonde (d. 1732). Grensholmen later passed on to his son, Lieutenant General, Count Axel Spens. This Count Spens, who was also a video commissioner to Engelholm, did not live there but at Grensholme
2 CONC n, where he only occupied two rooms in the former.  then the uninhabited main building. It is said that he, from the wars (see Engelholm) facing the hardships  of all kinds, even in advanced age never lived in eldadt room. The pro
2 CONC fit from the present condition of the farm is also to a significant extent also mentioned Count Spens, who at considerable cost executed planning, broke the yard and constructed roads and also erected many stone buildings of stone
2 CONC , regulated the estates, etc. For these magnificent works were used a few hundred maybe men, for which purposes were called here. These works went on as best at the time of the valley uprising in 1743. When rumored  if this dalall
2 CONC mogen's march to Stockholm got to Grensholmen, then even the suburban gentlemen who wanted to break up and take their dependents for support. It was difficult for Count Spens to impede the execution of this their decision. "But ou
2 CONC r agreement," he told them, "that every commencement of work must be completed, before you work, I will, however, stand by." 
2 CONT
2 CONT On the other hand, maybe the men had nothing to object to, but they decided to free themselves from their work with the first possible. Grefven, however, had in the meantime offered all the goods of his own people, and then perhap
2 CONC s
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source''' Project Runeberg - http://runeberg.org/osterlex/1/0168.html
