2 CONT ==[https://sok.riksarkivet.se/sbl/Presentation.aspx?id=13177 Branch Family]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Band 17 (1967-1969), page 258.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Branch, medieval Savior, whose family name can be dated to the earliest in 1424. Her oldest known ancestor, Kettilbjörn Knutsson (lived in 1323), was, according to several letters, a brother or, though less likely, half-brother to
2 CONC  the link-opian Björn (d. 1329 or 1330), of the years 1312-19. The former son Inge Kettilbjörnsson (still alive in 1335) became a knight shortly before his death. His brother Ernils Kettilbjörnsson (still alive in 1330), known as 
2 CONC one of those who in 1320 negotiated with King Magnus Eriksson's guardian government with King Birger's supporters at Viborg, became the father of Harald Ernilsson (still alive in 1360). He probably married a daughter from the Birg
2 CONC er Earl who was descended from the Birger Earl to the Swedish National Council Torkel Andersson (boat).
2 CONT
2 CONT Their son Torkel Haraldsson (d 1402) must in the 1370s for a drop make pilgrimages to Rome, Aachen and Nidaros, among others. Later, he was county governor in several herds in Södermanland and Östergötland. Torkel Haraldsson was d
2 CONC ubbed knight in the fall of 1389, probably at the men's day in Söderköping, where Queen Margareta took over power in Sweden, and was later named as the chief white man at Ämnisholm in Småland. In 1398 he was a member of King Erik'
2 CONC s Union Council, but he never belonged to the Swedish Council.
2 CONT
2 CONT His brother, the governor of the Austrians (South) Sten Haraldsson (still living in 1417) at Ål (now Grensholmen) in Vånga (Ög), became married to a daughter of King Erik Magnusson's counsel Nils Magnusson (Ivar Nilsson's ancestry
2 CONC ) father of the Council of Magnus G (G 1). Her son, the Swedish National Council Ivar G (G 2), became the father of the Swedish National Council Måns G (G 3). The G family extended with two of the latter's sisters, who were still 
2 CONC alive at the beginning of the 1540s. Among its descendants on the women's side was King Gustav I, whose maternal grandmother was brother to G 1. The family 
2 CONT
2 CONT name survives in the farm name Grensholmen. It was called Gl's farmhouse Ål from about 1510 by his son-in-law Erik Ryning, who then owned it.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source''' https://sok.riksarkivet.se/sbl/Presentation.aspx?id=13177
