2 CONC )''']
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sok.riksarkivet.se/?Sokord=Byting&EndastDigitaliserat=false&AvanceradSok=False&page=15&postid=sdhk_44755&tab=post&FacettState=undefined%3ac%7c#tab '''SDHK no: 44755''']
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Dating: 1479 without day''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT Content
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Byting Olofsson sells with his mother Humfrid Jonsdotter's consent land in Hög (Sunnersbergs sn, Kållands hd).'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Sources: Post-medieval regest/notice:  Kålland district court AIa:14 (1697-1700) (under 26 January 1698), GLA, Literature and commentary For Humfrid and son Byting, see SoH 1958, p. 10, and cf. SDHK no. 30430 , which letter this
2 CONC  (partially) supplemented/corrected dating is based on.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =Holmfrid Johansdotter Broka Notes=
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes 1 The letter of 25/7 1478 dated Stola has been partly misunderstood by both Almquist and Raneke. The former identifies the living mentioned son Byting with the seagull "Byting Olofsson" (which never existed), the latter inte
2 CONC rprets the text as if Tore Byting's widow Holmfrid Jonsdotter (who issued the donation letter) is already dead but on the other hand correctly interprets the son Byting as still alive . For copy of the letter I thank file. dr Hans
2 CONC  Gillingstam.
2 CONT
2 CONT An important letter, dated Stola 4/4 1452, has apparently been overlooked by research. From this it can be deduced that Holmfrid Jonsdotter was then a widow partly because she acts alone in business matters, partly because you fin
2 CONC d "God bless his soul" added after her husband Tore Byting's name. It is the husband's affairs with Mr. Åke Jönsson (Svarte Skåning, ÄSF I:3 p. 264), who had Åkerö in Södermanland as a seat farm, which is on the tape and a Jöns Br
2 CONC oka, "my close cousin", is authorized to manage them.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is possible that Holmfrid Jonsdotter entered into a new marriage around 1455 with the squire Björn Andersson from Lerum, because according to reliable information (see above) he then moved to Stola in Kålland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Raneke reports (aa. p. 255) a priest Jon Broka b. 1366–1407 with a severed human foot in the shield as belonging to Otterstad, where Holmfrid Jonsdotter apparently had his roots, and likewise (p. 353) a squire Jon Broka b. 1390 in
2 CONC  Otterstad with an eagle's foot in the shield. Perhaps Holmfrid's coat of arms with a four-legged animal(?) is in fact a misunderstood eagle's foot. Any of these could be her father. When Styffe (Scandinavia under the Union time 2
2 CONC . opp. p. 126) gives Holmfrid Jonsdotter's husband the name Jon Byting, he is apparently on the wrong track.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 He served as district chief 1465–1481 according to J E Almquist in Lagsagor og domsagor II p. 95.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 The personal name archive in Uppsala has information about a Claus Byting in Finland, who had a daughter Ingeborg married to Henrik Spore (Nådendal 7/3 1442). When this Ingeborg (Byting) was identified (Raneke aa. p. 396, Addend
2 CONC um) with the Ingeborg, who married in 1449 5/10 at Stola with Sven Jonsson to Dingelvik (half lily), this is obviously a mistake.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 See family tree ibid. p. 171. Henning Sollied, who died in 1945, was a very talented and conscientious researcher who above all devoted himself to Norway's medieval salvation. We can compare him with K H Karlsson in our country,
2 CONC  who in a short time, a few years around the turn of the century, processed almost the entire older Swedish nobility.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 Stola's ownership record during the 15th century in Swedish castles and manors (Vgl part I p. 435) only includes two names: Aron Bengtsson (Ulv) in the middle of the 15th century and Peter Larsson (Sparre) around 1480. Aron Beng
2 CONC tsson's daughter Ebba was married to the district chief and councilor Nils Klasson (Stolaätt, Raneke aa. p. 515) but there is no indication that her father would have owned land in Vgl. (ÄSF I:3 p. 304 f). On the other hand, Nils 
2 CONC Klasson had inherited Stola from his mother Karin Toresdotter (Byting), who probably took over the farm in the early 1480s, when his sister Ingegerd died and her husband Peder Bonde remarried with a daughter (whose name is unclear
2 CONC ) to Ture Jönsson Svarte Skåning (ÄSF I:3 p. 263 f) and moved to Påtorp in Vedens härad (SoH 1958 p. 11 tab. 18). According to ÄSF (I:3 p. 305), Nils Klasson who brought a white crescent in a blue field is known from 1504–1533. As
2 CONC  early as 1492, however, he is mentioned as a seal witness in a letter listed by Rasmus Ludvigsson among Per Nilsson's land deeds to Fly (B 22 p. 75 RA). That it really is Nils Klasson to Stola is evident from the seal with the cr
2 CONC escent moon, which is clearly marked in the margin. Otherwise, the identification is made more difficult by the fact that another Nils Klasson (Sparre av Ellinge) is active in Western Sweden at the same time, where this Scanian fa
2 CONC mily gained interests to guard through intermarriage with the older family Hjerne, which was possessed in Småland and Bohuslän (Norway). For the copies of Per Nilsson's land deed, I thank file. dr Hans Gillingstam and for informat
2 CONC ion on Tore Byting's Norwegian descendants file. stomach Cary Gustafsson.
2 CONT
2 CONT Regarding the length of ownership of Stola during the 15th century, a "Sven i Stola" figures around the middle of the century. He is called the grandfather of the courtier Otte Torbjörnsson (bird) at Älvsborg, who was executed in 
2 CONC 1475 on charges of privateering (Jan Liedgren: Kammarmästaren Ingemar Ragvaldsson - - Sveriges Släktforskareförbund's yearbook 1988 p. 237). Could he be identical to the Sven Jonsson in Dingelvik (half lily) who in 1449 married In
2 CONC geborg Toresdotter (Byting) at Stola? In that case, three seagulls to Tore Byting i.e. one after the other have signed up for Stola: 1) Sven Jonsson (half lily) g.m. Ingeborg 2) Per Bonde (Sparre af Rossvik) g.m. Ingegerd 3) Claus
2 CONC  Ribbing (crescent) g.m. Karin
2 CONT
