1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Bengta Ragnvaldsdotter Puke=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Daughter of Ragnvald Johannesson Puke, i Kosjöhult and Katarina Karlsdotter Lejonbalk
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sok.riksarkivet.se/sbl/Mobil/Artikel/7422 Puke Family, by the Swedish Biographical Dictionary]
2 CONT
2 CONT The family name survived, however, in that Ragvald Erengisleson (d. sometime between 1381 and 1384), one of the sons of Filip Ragvaldsson and Kettil P's sister Benedicta Ragvaldsdotter (d. not earlier than 1350) in her marriage to
2 CONC  Erengisle Petersson (Bonde ; vol. 5, p. 311), sometimes called himself Ragvald P. He was named as Norwegian councilor in the 1371 peace treaty between kings Håkan and Albrekt, and Albrekt confiscated his Swedish estate (Engström)
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT His son Peter Ragvaldsson(d sometime between 1424 and 1428) became the father of three sons named P. One of them, Ragvald P (d no earlier than 1449), participated in the meeting in Vadstena in 1436, where a truce was concluded bet
2 CONC ween King Erik's representative and the Swedish royal council. According to an entry deleted during the final editing of the Karlskrönikan, he is said to have been one of the two men who, through his revelation to Karl Knutsson, b
2 CONC ecame the triggering factor for the execution of the rebel leader Erik P (cf. below) in 1437. Ragvald P also participated in the union meeting in Kalmar in 1438, the lord's day in Arboga in 1440 and the Kalmar meeting in 1441, but
2 CONC  the genealogical table entry that he was a royal councilor is incorrect (Löfqvist). At least from 1441 to 1448 (cf. vol. 27, p. 56) he was, however, a lawman in Östergötland, and in the spring of 1449 (Sparre; cf. perg:brev) he b
2 CONC ecame a knight. This cognatic branch of the P family died out on the male side with P's only son, who was named Peder Bonde (d. 1469) and fell in the battle of Herrljunga in Västergötland. The latter's sister Ingeborg Ragvaldsdott
2 CONC er was married to the councilor and lawman in Södermanland Peder Ragvaldsson (Fargalt; vol. 15). Ragvald P's nephew was the dean of Linköping, Tord Petersson, also called Tord Bonde or Tord Bonde Petersson, who shortly before his 
2 CONC death in 1470 was postulated to be the archbishop of Uppsala. The rebel leader Erik P (see below), who was the son of the royal councilor Nils Gustavsson (Rossviksätten) and was apparently named after the aforementioned Erik Ketti
2 CONC lsson or Erik P , has recently been wrongly identified with the lawyer Ragvald P's brother Erik P (d. no earlier than 1444). At the same time, there was also an Östergötland farmer named Erik P.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sok.riksarkivet.se/sbl/Mobil/Artikel/7422#/sbl/Mobil/Artikel/17914 Farmer Relative, by Swedish Biographical Dictionary]
2 CONT
2 CONT Bonde, along with the families Bielke and Natt och Dag, is the oldest Swedish noble family of purely domestic origin that still survives. Possibly the knight Torer Bonde , who sometime between 1282 and 1286 witnessed the confirmat
2 CONC ion of a donation made to the monastery in Vreta Monastery and whose death date (but not year of death) is recorded in the calendar of the Stockholm Grey Monk Monastery, was already a member of the family; he is in any case the ea
2 CONC rliest nobleman with the surname Bonde that appears in the sources. However, the documentary evidence of the family line is based on the brothers Tord B. (1310−27) and Erengisle Petersson(1319−50), and since there is no reason for
2 CONC  the opinion sometimes expressed in the literature that these were only half-brothers and had only a common mother, we have to see in Peter (Bonde?), whose existence is evident from Erengisle's father's name, the ancestor of both 
2 CONC brothers' descendants and thus of the entire Bonde family. We know nothing else about him; A hypothesis put forward that he is identical with a person in Finveden and Värend named Peter Erengislesson, who already in 1272, probably
2 CONC  on his deathbed, draws up his will, gives room for such strong doubts of a chronological and other nature that it can barely even be maintained as a guess, and the alternative assumption made that he was the son of the Tord Peter
2 CONC sson mentioned in a letter from 1259, who apparently belonged to the noble class, and that he would thus have been called Peter Tordsson, is, although somewhat more probable, little more than a loose conjecture, based as it is sol
2 CONC ely on the fact that the names Tord and Peter were common within the Bonde family during the 14th century. The traditional view that the family originates from Småland — in the northernmost part of this province, a family member a
2 CONC lready owned the farm Bordsjö (Askeryd parish, Norra Vedbo district) in the mid-14th century, which is still in the family's possession — rests on rather uncertain grounds, and the opinion that the place of origin is in fact to be
2 CONC  sought in Östergötland is probably of about equal validity; both Tord Bonde's and Erengisle Petersson's family branches had estates in the western part of the latter province during the earlier 14th century, while from the beginn
2 CONC ing the former branch, as far as is known, completely or at least almost completely lacked such in Småland. The family has throughout its historically known existence carried a boat (usually placed across the shield but during the
2 CONC  14th century at some point diagonally across it) in its coat of arms. The same coat of arms was used from the end of the 13th century or the beginning of the 14th century by several other Småland and Östergötland families and lin
2 CONC eages, and among them at least two were apparently related to the Bonde family, although the nature of the relationship cannot now be determined; perhaps both of these families were simply branches of the same family as the Bonde 
2 CONC family, in which case the differentiation must have occurred in the middle of the 13th century or somewhat later. One was represented by the knights Sigmund Erengislesson (1296−1321) and Torkel Erengislesson Djäkn (1299−1311), who
2 CONC  were apparently brothers, and by the latter's son (?) Erengisle Torkelsson (1325−29). The second family was again represented in its first now known generation by two brothers, Sune and Peter Jonssöner, both knights and both unde
2 CONC r Magnus Eriksson.government politically active (cf. the article Bååt, family tree); the hypothesis of this family's close kinship with the Bonde family is supported, among other things, by the fact that the aforementioned Peter J
2 CONC onsson bears the surname Bonde in a seal used in 1340. Erengisle Bonde, who appears in documents in the years 1389−92, was probably also from the same lineage as Peter Jonsson and his brother; he runs the boat and is called a kins
2 CONC man of Sune Jonsson's son, Earl Erengisle Sunesson. — The above-mentioned brothers Tord B. and Erengisle Petersson each founded their own branch of the family, of which the former originated before the end of the Middle Ages but i
2 CONC n return, line after line, took a place within the highest aristocracy and finally gave the country a king, while the Erengisle line, which still survives, during the first three centuries of its existence only counted a few more 
2 CONC prominent members and only in the 17th century entered its real heyday. A ) Tord Bonde's line : The ancestor, Tord Bonde himself, is called a knight from 1316 and became a councilor of the kingdom no later than 1320. During the ti
2 CONC me of King Birger he was obviously close to the dukes — as Duke Erik's husband he appears as early as 1310 — and after the Nyköping banquet he took part in the general uprising against Birger. He later attended the lord's day in S
2 CONC tockholm in June 1320 and was thus complicit in the execution of the young squires Magnus, which according to the most credible information (Hälsingelagens ärvdabalk, XVI; cf. Dipl. suec, 3, p. 647) took place at this meeting and 
2 CONC not at the time usually assumed on the authority of the Erikskrönikan in October of that year. In 1322 he joined the confederation of lords in Skara directed against Duchess Ingeborg . Living in Östergötland, where in 1316 he had 
2 CONC the manor house Örbäck (probably in Brunneby parish, Bobergs härad), he was also, probably as a result of his marriage, a landowner also in Södermanland and in this capacity came to join the committee that drew up the Södermannala
2 CONC gen in the mid-1320s. He seems to have died not long after King Magnus (10 Aug. 1327) ratified the law thus created; in 1330 he is explicitly mentioned as deceased. Married to Margareta Röriksdotter (snedbjälke), a Frankish woman 
2 CONC of the royal family, he left behind sons Peter B . (1340−62) and Rörik B . (1341−64), both knights. The former was a councilor in the early 1360s, and the latter also seems to have held the councilorship. Peter B. left no son behi
2 CONC nd him; Rörik, on the other hand, became the father of the knight and councilor Tord Röriksson B. , d. 1417 (see below 1). Two sons of the latter reached adulthood: the knight Knut B., d. c. 1412 (more about him in his father's bi
2 CONC ography), and the councilor Karl Tordsson B. , d. c. 1443 (see below 2). Knut B's only son was King Karl Knutsson(see this one), b. probably 1408 (Sv. medeltidens rimkrönikor, 2, p. 178), d. 1470; Karl Tordsson B. again had two so
2 CONC ns, the famous marshal Tord B. , d. 1456 (see below 3), and the knight Bo Karlsson Djure , d. 1465 (more details about him in the biography of the father), neither of whom left male offspring. The family line originated on the mal
2 CONC e side with King Karl's son Karl Karlsson , who was admittedly born illegitimately but was recognized by the royal father, when he legitimized his relationship with his mother Kristina Abrahamsdotter on his deathbed, as a legitima
2 CONC te son with full rights of inheritance. According to a 16th-century author, Olaus Magnus , he is said to have been a promising youth (optimas spei adolescens) but nevertheless came to live and age in a low and despised position, b
2 CONC ecause after his father's death and in violation of the provisions he made on his deathbed, he was still considered illegitimate and was therefore deprived of his paternal inheritance. Whether he really reached such an advanced ag
2 CONC e, as the referenced statement suggests, must be considered doubtful; that he was still alive on 24 Sept. 1488, a note in the Stockholm Thought Book for the said day seems to indicate. But in all other respects, Olaus Magnus' info
2 CONC rmation about him given above seems to be largely correct; that he essentially became disinherited is confirmed by contemporary sources. B ) Erengisle Petersson's line : Erengisle Petersson, who unlike his brother neither became a
2 CONC  knight nor a councillor and never played any political role, was a resident of Värend, where he had the manor of Kedjenäs (Kianäs) in Älghult parish. He was last mentioned on 30 March 1350, when he and his wife Bengta and their c
2 CONC hildren agreed that the couple's only daughter in paternal and maternal inheritance would receive an equal share of their land in Värend and other parts of what was then southern Sweden as each of the three sons, and in connection
2 CONC  with this gave detailed provisions about the four lots. Since it cannot be a coincidence that the deed of inheritance in question originates from a family living in Värend and mainly concerns properties located there, the deed sh
2 CONC ould be considered as an early testimony — the oldest preserved — of the Värend inheritance practice struggling with Earl Birger's inheritance law, which has otherwise only been traced in the sources from the 17th century onwards.
2 CONC  Of Erengisle's sons, Peter Erengislesson B . till Bordsjö (d. ca. 1390) and Ragvald Erengislesson Puke participated(d. no later than 1393) on the side of the Folkungs in their wars with King Albrekt and the Mecklenburg party in S
2 CONC weden 1363−71, while the third brother Filip Erengislesson B. to Vanborga on Öland, who was last mentioned in 1367, apparently fought on the opposite side; none of the three brothers achieved the dignity of knight or councillor. R
2 CONC agvald's surname Puke is likely to have been adopted from his maternal lineage. It did not pass to his son, squire Peter Ragvaldsson (Bonde) , who died in the 1420s, but was instead carried by the latter's sons, among them the roy
2 CONC al councillor and the Östgöta magistrate Ragvald Petersson Puke (knight at Karl Knutsson's coronation in 1448, died around 1450), only to be abandoned again by the next generation, represented by the younger Ragvald Puke's son, sq
2 CONC uire Peter Ragvaldsson B. , who fell in the Battle of Herrljunga in 1469 and was the last member of this Puke branch within the Bonde family. Peter Erengislesson B. to Bordsjö became the father of Tord Petersson B. , d. 1412, who 
2 CONC as the first of Erengisle Petersson's line received the knighthood at the royal tribute at Mora äng in 1396 but was probably not a member of the royal council, and of the squire Filip Petersson B., district governor in Vedbo, d. d
2 CONC uring the first half of the 1440s. The latter attended a few lords' days and meetings in the years 1434−36 but was otherwise not politically active. One of Filip's sons, the squire Magnus Filipsson B. to Bordsjö, district governor
2 CONC  in Norra Vedbo, d. in the early 1490s, was completely outside political life, apart from the fact that he, probably in 1471 and in the ranks of the national party, participated in a siege of Stäkehölm (Scriptores rerum Suecicarum
2 CONC , III: 2, p. 267). The second son, however, Tord Filipsson B ., d 1484 or 1485 (residing on Säm in Västergötland), was, along with the aforementioned Östgötalaggmann Ragvald Petersson Puke, the only medieval member of Erengisle Pe
2 CONC tersson's lineage who worked in a more prominent position in the field of state life. Knight at Christian I's coronation in 1457 and at the latest in 1463 councilor, he stood under Christian's banner at the outbreak of Bishop Kett
2 CONC il's rebellion in 1464 (A. 20, f. 199, RA) but joined King Karl Knutsson after his return to Sweden, s. å.; from then on he was a frequent participant in the council meetings until his death. Since he left no son and since the bra
2 CONC nch of the family to which King Karl Knutsson belonged died out during the last half century of the Middle Ages, his aforementioned brother Magnus B. became the ancestor of all members of the Bondeätten family in the following. Hi
2 CONC s son Tord Magnusson B. to Traneberg (Västergötland), d. 1545 or 1546, was summoned to the Reichsrat at the 1523 Electoral Parliament in Strängnäs and appointed as the magistrate of Tiohärad. He was in the next few years in the po
2 CONC ssession of Gustav Vasa's favor and received the Mohärad in Småland as a fief, but in 1529, due to participation in the rebellion of the lords of Västergötland, he lost both the offices of Reichsrat and magistrate as well as the f
2 CONC ief and was further punished with a hefty fine. His political role was thus forever played out; only as a member of the commoners did he attend the Riksdag in Västerås in 1544. His son Jöns B . to Traneberg and Stensholm (Västergö
2 CONC tland), d. 1566, received knighthood at the coronation of Erik XIV, was summoned to the royal council at about the same time and served at the beginning of the Nordic Seven Years' War as commander on the Västergötland side, where 
2 CONC in the autumn of 1563 he led the invasion of Bohuslän and Viken together with Per Brahe. He became the father of Tord B. to Stensholm, d. 1628, and to Duke Charles's council Philip B. to Säckestad (Västergötland), d. 1588. The for
2 CONC mer was accused by Charles IX of treasonous stampings in favor of King Sigismund and sentenced to death in 1604: pardoned from the death penalty, he was kept in prison during the remaining years of King Charles' reign and only reg
2 CONC ained his freedom in 1612. His family branch (Stensholmsgrenen) died out on the male side in 1716. Filip B's sons Karl, Jöns and Ulf B. became the ancestors of each branch of the Bonde family: a ) Karl Bonde's branch (Laihelagrene
2 CONC n) : Karl B. , councillor, d. 1652 (see below 4), was raised to the status of baron in 1651 with Laihela parish in Ostrobothnia as a baronship and became the ancestor of the baronial family B. to Laihela (died as baron in 1712). O
2 CONC f his sons, the treasurer of the kingdom, Baron Gustaf B. , d. 1667 (see below 5), and the councilor of the kingdom, Baron Krister B. , d. 1659 (see below 6), the former became the father of the councilor Karl B. , d. 1699 (see be
2 CONC low 7), who in 1695 received the dignity of count and became the ancestor of the still living count family of Bonde af Björnö. The sons of Count Karl B. were the councilor of the kingdom, Count Gustaf B. , d. 1764 (see below 9), a
2 CONC nd the governor, Count Nils B. , d. 1760 (see below 10). The former became the father of the royal councilor Count Carl B. , d. 1791 (see below 11), whose son, one of the lords of the realm Count Gustaf B. , d. 1855 (see below 13)
2 CONC , as holder of the Trolleholm trust received royal permission in 1809 to call himself Trolle-Bonde, which name has since been borne by the trust commissioners to Trolleholm. This Count Gustaf Trolle-Bonde's nephew was the lieutena
2 CONC nt-colonel Count Gustaf Trolle-B. , d. 1884, (see below 14), whose other son Count Carl Trolle-B., b 1912 (see below 17), after him Trolleholm held. The current holder of the trust is the latter's son, the chamberlain Count Gustaf
2 CONC  Trolle-B ., b. 1868 (d 1951). b ) Jöns Bondes branch (Borrö and Fituna branch , so named after the family estates Borrö, now Borrad, in Västergötland and Fituna on Södertörn): Jöns B. (assessor in the Göta Court of Appeal, d 1662
2 CONC ) was the great-grandfather of the lieutenant-colonel, one of the lords of the realm Carl Göran B. , d 1840 (see below 12), who in 1802 was elevated to the rank of baron and in 1803 as baron was introduced into the extinct baronia
2 CONC l family B:s to Laihela number. Of his grandsons, the Lord Chamberlain Baron Carl Jedvard B. , d. 1895 (see below 15) and the Lord Chamberlain Junker Baron Knut Filip B. , d. 1871 (see below 16), the former became the father of th
2 CONC e Lord Chamberlain Junker and Speaker Baron Carl Carlson B. , d. 1913 (see below 18). The latter's son, Baron Carl Gotthard B. , b. 1877, currently holds the fideicommisse Eriksberg, which has belonged to the Baronial family of B 
2 CONC since 1808. c ) Ulf Bonde's branch (Säckestadsbranch) : Ulf B. (colonel, d. 1657) was the father of Ulf B . (b. 1635, d. 1687), who became an assessor in the commercial college and land judge (lawman) in Skåne and in the house of 
2 CONC knights and in the great commission against the radical reduction and räfst party, represented a more moderate position; he was also known for his good understanding of "antiquities and genealogy of Sweden". A son of his was the c
2 CONC ouncilor Claes B. , d. 1726 (see below 8), who was elevated to the rank of count in 1719. His count family, Bonde af Sävstaholm, ceased to exist in 1783 with his son Fredrik Lorens B. , who was also the last male member of the ent
2 CONC ire Säckestads branch. — The Bondeska family's main family archive is named after Sävstaholm, where it was kept before it (1911) was deposited in the national archives. Its core consists of papers belonging to the aforementioned J
2 CONC öns B. of Traneberg and Stensholm (d. 1566) and his descendants of the Säckestad and Laihela branches; however, the papers of both family branches must have suffered losses in the Hässelby fire on March 4, 1721 and the Sävstaholm 
2 CONC fire on February 28, 1762. The Sävstaholm archive has been listed in the 36th part of »Handlingar rörande Skandinaviens historia» (1855) and in the 4th and 7th parts of »Historiska handlingar» (1864, 1870). Of a less uniform chara
2 CONC cter but equally extremely important for historical research is the magnificent collection of papers from above all various family and manor archives, which, thanks to a collecting spirit inherited through generations, has been br
2 CONC ought together on the Borrö and Fituna branches' estate Eriksberg.
2 CONT
