2 CONT B. had long expected that in a revelation, God's command to make a pilgrimage to the Holy Land. This also came in May 1371. However, the journey did not come until the beginning of 1372. The reason for the delay was B's desire to 
2 CONC bring his two sons along. After 1369, they had returned to Sweden, but now they returned to Italy and joined the pilgrimage, to which the daughter Katarina, the two scriptural fathers Peter Olovsson, knight Magnus Persson of Eka, 
2 CONC who long belonged to B's circle in Rome (later Vadstena monastery) first general confessor after masters Peter Olovsson), bishop Alfons et al. The road went over Naples, where B. was received by Queen Johanna with great honors. B.
2 CONC  now imagined his sons to the Queen. About this, Margareta Klausdotter tells us an often-quoted story, who may have some foundation in reality. While Birger kissed the queen's foot in a high-fashioned manner, Karl also kissed her 
2 CONC on her mouth. The consequence will have been a great love of the queen to the stranger from the Nordic countries, aimed at marriage, even though Karl was married and also Johanna's third man was probably also in life. However, Kar
2 CONC l became ill and died in Naples, and B. had a revelation of his condition after death (VII: 13). The journey continued, and near a month's residency was made in Cyprus, an independent kingdom, which was then ruled by a Queen Eleon
2 CONC ora of Aragon, who received B. as a heavenly envoy to her help. In early May, the pilgrims arrived in the Holy Land. You landed in Joppe, where the ship suffered shipwreck in the harbor itself, whereby a large part of the effects 
2 CONC brought about were lost. Arrived in Jerusalem in mid-May, Birger was dubbed the knight of the holy grave (according to Margareta Klausdotter and Diarium Vazstenense). Other places in the Holy Land were also visited, among others. 
2 CONC a. Bethlehem. But the hardship of the journey and the heat of the summer of summer finally seem to have broken B's forces, so that she fell ill in fever. Well, she picked up something, so that home travel after four and a half mon
2 CONC ths' stay in Palestine could be undertaken. The home journey was again over Cyprus and Naples, where they were at the beginning of 1373. From here she sent the Pope another letter, with Alfon's departure to Avignon. The stay in Na
2 CONC ples was slightly delayed by a lack of funds for departure. B. then received a substantial sum of money from Queen Johanna, which she received after some hesitation. In March, the pilgrims were again in Rome after something more t
2 CONC han a year's absence. And here B. started his usual div with church visits and devotional exercises. She received an envoy from Gregory, who was now really planning to move to Rome, and sent him another final letter.
