1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Ragvald Magnusson (Fargalt)=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of Magnus Ragvaldsson the Squire (Fargalt) and Margareta Karlsdotter
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sok.riksarkivet.se/?Sokord=Fargalt&EndastDigitaliserat=false&AvanceradSok=False&page=17&postid=sdhk_25089&tab=post SDHK-nr: 25089]
2 CONT
2 CONT * Dating: 1447 February 26, Place of issue: Strangnes
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Ragvald Magnusson (Fargalt) sells with the consent of Katarina Svensdotter (Pik)''' to wife Birgitta land in Mörby and Åkerby in Turinge parish in Öknebo district, which he inherited from his wife's mother, wife Cecilia, who wa
2 CONC s married to Sven Pik.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Sources: Medieval Transcripts: Vid. 1448 4/4 paper, SDHK no. 25315, National Archives, Post-Medieval Transcripts: Misc. 50:34, Literature and commentary, See DMS, Södermanland 2:6, p. 214 f. and p. 222.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sok.riksarkivet.se/?Sokord=sdhk+18668&EndastDigitaliserat=false&AvanceradSok=False&page=1&postid=sdhk_18668&tab=post SDHK-nr: 18668]
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Dating: 1416 January 7, Place of issue: Pagan island'''
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://sok.riksarkivet.se/dokument/sdhk/18668.pdf '''Magnus Ragvaldsson and his wife Margareta Karlsdotter donate land to Vadstena monastery in Höglunda in Kalmar sn, Håbo hd.''']
2 CONT
2 CONT It is to be known to all, as is the case with the present time, that I, Magnus Ragualzson, and my wife Margareta Karlsdotter, may open a small house with these things and give them to God, my wife and Saint Birgitta to the monaste
2 CONC ry of Vazstena for our souls and all our ancestors' souls, as we have been obliged to do, and for all Christian souls, half a piece of land in Lodherstom and half a piece of land in Hughlund in the Kalmar parish in Habo hundare an
2 CONC d in the bishopric of Opsala, and that we will leave you and leave this prescribed land and the noble Vazstena monastery with all the additions inside and outside, and that you vndantaghno, som thøm nw ælla aff aller tillighat hau
2 CONC er, huat nampne thet hælzt næmpnas kan, epthà dagh til ewerdhelica ægho, oc giwom hæredzhøfdhinganom i forscrefno hæredhe fulla mact medh thæsso breue clostersens visso budh thæsso jordher fasta oc fastfara epthà thy som Swerichis
2 CONC  lagh vtuisa. Til mere visso oc større beuaring latom we var incighle medh vilia oc witzscap hængia til thtta breff oc bedhoms til vitnesbyrdh vælborna manna incighle, swasom herra Karl Magnussons oc herra Niclisa Gøtzstafsons kni
2 CONC ghts, fore the thtta breff met varom eghnom incighlom. Scriptum Hedhensøø, anno Domini millesimo cd°xvj°, tercia feria infra octauam epiphanie Domini
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Sources:''' Original: Or. perg. RA 0101 images, Post-medieval copies: Peringskiöld: E 60:A f. 35v (reg.); Örnhielm: IX p. 1299
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sok.riksarkivet.se/sbl/Presentation.aspx?id=15148 Fargalt Family, by the Swedish Biographical Dictionary]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Volume 15 (1956), page 367.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Fargalt, conventional, after the coat of arms ("fargalt", i.e. wild boar) formed family name of a medieval royal family, which already early had significant landholdings, gradually came to belong to the high royal family and was m
2 CONC ainly connected to Södermanland. The tinctures of the coat of arms are stated in a painted coat of arms in Tövsala church (Åbo o. Björneb. county) as black and yellow (Hausen, 1882, no. 78, cf. the preface), by Peringskiöld as a b
2 CONC lack fargalt in a field of gold.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family descends from the squire Ragvald Karlsson , who seals in 1334 in Östergötland (DS no. 3084; seal) and in 1345 in Södermanland (DS no. 4011). Judging by the seal, father's name and place of residence, the squire Nils Rag
2 CONC valdsson (sealed in 1342 according to Genealogica 75, fol. 241 v, RA [cf. Register å dokument, Misc. 10: 53 HA: s dep., RA] and 1345, DS no. 3954, also mentioned in 1359, RPB nos. 417, 418) is his son. He had three sons Ragvald, A
2 CONC nders and Magnus Nilsson, of whom the first two were excommunicated in 1358 (RPB no. 365; about Magnus cf. Geneal. 41, fol. 204 v, RA). The first-mentioned squire Ragvald Nilsson (b. c. 1328) was district governor in Oppunda (Söd.
2 CONC ) in 1355 (DS no. 5123) and again in 1361 (RPB 506) and owned Gräfseboda (now Haneberg) and Hedensö (both in Näshulta sn, Söd.). He is particularly known through Birgitta's canonization process (ed. by Collijn), which tells that R
2 CONC agvald had a stroke on Hedensö on 23 Aug. 1374, during which he became distorted in the face and paralyzed in half his body. After it was determined by a roll of the dice that one should call upon the already then considered saint
2 CONC  Birgitta Birgersdotter , Ragvald made a pilgrimage vow, the paralysis began to recede, and he made a pilgrimage to Vadstena, where he was seen "completely healthy, rosy in the face and with a well-fed body". The rumor about the m
2 CONC iracle spread far and wide. He was still alive in 1388 (RPB no. 2378). His wife Ingegerd Karlsdotter was the daughter of the squire Karl Dansson (Stjärnbjälke) and Ingeborg Magnusdotter (illegitimate Folkungaätten). Ragvald had pr
2 CONC obably inherited Hedensö in Österrekarne from his father-in-law. Ragvald Nilsson had a daughter and two sons, the prebendary of Strängnäs Karl Ragvaldsson (mentioned 1382–1401) and the squire Magnus Ragvaldsson(mentioned 1399–1419
2 CONC ). The latter owned Hedensö (SD no. 570) and Gräfseboda (E 176, fol. 11 r, UB), as well as other land in Södermanland, Östergötland and Uppland. He was buried on 6 Feb. 1419 in Vadstena monastery church (in the Vadstena diary he i
2 CONC s then called a squire from Uppland, 'armiger de Uplandia'), together with his wife, who died a short time before him. Their son was the squire Ragvald Magnusson, who is mentioned from 1426 (28 April, perg., RA) and was still aliv
2 CONC e in 1469 (Hist. handl., 16: 1, p. 28, note 68). He owned Hedensö and Västerby in Tumbo (Västerrekarne; Hadorph's large copybook E 73–76, machine folio 191 v; Örnhielm, Dipl. 11, p. 304), as well as Herrborum in Skällviks sn (Ög.)
2 CONC  – probably his wife's inheritance or morning gift (perg. 2 and 19 Jan. 1463, RA) and farms in Västgoeda and Närkingen, which were his wife's inheritance. He was married to Katarina Svensdotter, daughter of the squire Sven Pik (Pe
2 CONC rsonhist. tidskr., 1909, p. 19). They had several children, including the squires, councilors and lawmen Peder Ragvaldsson (B. 1), mentioned 1463–99, and Erik Ragvaldsson (B. 2), mentioned 1463–94, and Karl Ragvaldsson, bailiff at
2 CONC  Turku Castle, mentioned 1463–72 (perg. 2 Jan. 1463, RA; FMU, 4, no. 3487); the coat of arms of the latter is painted in Tövsala church (Hausen, 1873, pp. 28 f., and 1882, preface and no. 78). With Peder Ragvaldsson the family see
2 CONC ms to have become extinct on the male side. He left behind two daughters (Anna Pedersdotter Bielke's family book, fol. 32 v, Riddarhuset; perg. 9 July 1519, RA), Margareta Pedersdotter , gm squire Klas Kyle, and Karin Pedersdotter
2 CONC  , gm (her sister's brother-in-law) squire, finally the lawman on Öland Johan Kyle.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT —
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Armsman Ragvald Magnusson sells to the well-born Knut Jönsson his wife's fathers, namely 2 waste plots, one in Rånnum and one, "Krecklingge", on Vänersnäs, as well as a mill stream between Halle- and Hunneberg, for 1 read barley a
2 CONC nd 3 Arnold gold, against conditions, that the seller travels to Västergötland to appoint men who can view and measure these goods. On 7 October 1435. (Vid. 1551 8/2 in B 19 c no. 179 RA 0202)
2 CONT
2 CONT Ragvald Magnusson sells with the consent of Katarina Svensdotter to wife Birgitta land in Mörby and Åkerby in Turinge parish in Öknabo district, which he inherited from his wife mother, wife Cecilia, who was married to Sven Piik. 
2 CONC Strängnäs on 26 February 1447 (Vid. 1448 4/4 papp. RA)
2 CONT
2 CONT Ragvald Magnusson (fargalt) and his wife Katrina Svensdotter give their farm "Hærboholm" (now Herrborum) in Skällviks sn (Hammarkinds hd) to their son Magnus Ragvaldsson, if he marries maiden Katarina Bengtsdotter, as long as the 
2 CONC mother was alive and after her death with full ownership rights to deny her heirs other estates for their right of inheritance in the estate. Härboholm on 19 October 1463. (Or.perg. RA 0101)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =Links=
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://sok.riksarkivet.se/sbl/Presentation.aspx?id=15148
2 CONT
2 CONT =Sources and Literature=
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: cited parchment letters RA and Hadorph's large copybook E 73–76, Örnhielm's Diplomatarium, 11, and Register å dokument, HA:s dep., RA; Anna Pedersdotter Bielke's family book and KH Karlsson's Biographical notes for the Mi
2 CONC ddle Ages and E. Anthonis' genealogy Fargalt, Riddarhuset; Gustaf Rosenhane's copybook (sign. E. 176) and C. Annerstedt, Svenska medeltidens frälsesläkter (sign. X 58:a–h), UB. – Acta et processus canonizacionis beate Birgitte, ed
2 CONC . by I. Collijn (1924–31), especially pp. 182 f.; Bidrag till Skandinaviens hist., ed. by CG Styffe, 4 (1875); Diplomatarium suecanum, 4–6 (1853–56, 1858–65, 1878–1946); Diarium Vazstenense (Scriptores rerum svecicarum, 1: 1, 1818
2 CONC ), p. 140; BE Hildebrand, Sv. sigiller från medeltiden (1862–67); Sv. diplomatarium, 1–3 (1875–1902); Sv. medeltidsregester, ed. by S. Tunberg et al. (1937); Sv. riks-archivets pergamentsbrev, 1–3 (1866–72). – JA Almquist, Frälseg
2 CONC odsen i Sverige under storhetstiden, 3: 2 (1947), p. 792; R. Hausen, Notes made during an antiquarian trip in the summer of 1871 in Southern Finland and on Åland (1873); [dens.], Afbildningar af vapensköldar fordom uppsätta i Finl
2 CONC ands kyrkor, ed. by R. Hausen (1882), foreword and no. 78; B. Hildebrand, Esbjörn Blåpanna and his heirs (Personhist. tidskr., 35, 1934, tr. 1935); KH Karlsson, Genealogical notes, 4. Medieval families with the name Pik (Personhis
2 CONC t. tidskr., 11, 1909), p. 19. – Reported by professor E. Anthoni and phil. dr. H. Gillingstam.
