1 OCCU Mill Maker / 1656 First Chief Justice of the Swedes' Court, Soldier, Millmaker - Shipped "Charitas", Judge
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/details/M5R2-HNF
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.colonialswedes.org/forefathers/Stille.html
2 CONT
2 CONT http://groups.google.com/group/soc.genealogy.medieval/browse_thread/thread/156c87a85d87f1ec/e14a67d4cc3f046a
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olof_Persson_Stille Wikipedia biography]
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~debyj/My%20Files/Desc%20Hans%20Mounson.html Olof Persson Stille & Hans Månsson ] 
2 CONT
2 CONT To engange in agriculture, paid at the start 50 daler, copper money, drawing no additional wages, but to be paid for whatever work he does for, and for whatever he furnishes to, the Company. His place of residence is indicated on 
2 CONC Lindsrom's Map. In 1658 he was one of the magistrates on the Delaware. He was still living in July, 1684, when he obtained confirmation of a grant of land in Passyunk, in Philadelpha County, PA (ceded to him June 3, 1664, by d'Hin
2 CONC ojossa, the Dutch Governor, on "South River"), but died before May, 1693, leaving as heir an only son, John Stille, born in America in 1646, the ancestor of a well known Philadelphia family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Olaf Stille arrived on the Charitas November 7, 1641 with his wife and daughter Ella Stillemons whom I am also descended from; brother, Axel, and Mons Svenson Lom and his wife Anna Petersdotter likely sister of Axel & Olaf accordi
2 CONC ng to Dr Stebbins Craig.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Olof Persson Stille immigrated in 1641 to New Sweden. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Olaf Stille', a soldier of Queen Christiana, arrived at Fort Christian, New Sweden with two children on February 9, 1643 - see Hacord's Annals of Pennsylvania, footnote by Rev. Dr. Collin. Olaf. in 1658, was appointed by the Dutch
2 CONC  to deal with the Indians; and later he settled between Ridley and Cram Creeks in Delaware County, Pennsylvania, on a farm called Techorehasse (Techoherasse) where he d. in 1666.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some descendants took the name Steelman.
2 CONT
2 CONT RECORD:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Peter Stebbins Craig and Henry Wesley Yocom, Yocums of Aronameck in Philadelphia, 1648 - 1702, The (National Genealogical Quarterly Vol. 71 No. 3 December 1983), pg. 248, 251-2, 273. NOTE: Swedish Colonial News Vol. 1 No. 15 (S
2 CONC pring 1997)  ?updated version??. 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Olof Stille,  who departed for New Sweden in 1641 with his wife and two small children, aged seven and one and a half. The birth year of the eldest, 1634, conforms to the description of Ella Steelman's age at her death. That both
2 CONC  children were girls seems to be confirmed by the fact that Olof Stille's only  known son, John Stille, was not born until 1646. Indeed, Olof's eldest daughter was virtually the only marriagble woman in New Sweden around 1652 at t
2 CONC he time Peter Jochimson must have married. The there is Ella's selection of the surname Steelman for herself and her sons by Hans Mansson after his death..... 
2 CONT
2 CONT Olof Stille arrived in New Sweden with his family on the Charitias in 1641. Achieving early prominence in New Sweden, he was called upon by the Governor Printz to deliver a protest to the Dutch in 1646. Soon thereafter he fell out
2 CONC  of favor with the governor. In 1648 Stille purchased a malf calf from Rev. Johan Campanius Holm....
2 CONT
2 CONT Stille not only was one of the 22 signers of the 1653 complaint against Prinz but also, as  we have seen, was accused by Printz as being one of the three ringleaders in this "mutiny". After Rising's arrival Stille signed the 1654 
2 CONC oath of allegiance. Under Dutch rule he served as one of the first magistrates for the "up-river" settlers in present Pennsylvania. Initially the Stille family settled at a place called Tequirassy next to present Ridley Creek, the
2 CONC n called "Olle Stillen's Kill". Governor Printz in 1653 described this settlement as consisting of three plantations with 12 morgans (25 acres) of cultivated land. Under Dutch rule Olof Stille moved to Moyamensing next to WIcaco. 
2 CONC HIs intial patent for this land was issued 3 June 1664. The last discovered mention of his name occured 21 July 1684 when WIlliam Penn reissued the patent to the four Moyamensing landowners, William (Olof) Stille, Andrew Bnakson (
2 CONC Andreas Bengtsson), Lasse Andrews (Lars Andersson Collins) and John Matson. Olof Stille must have died shortly thereafter, as Holme's map of Pennsylvania landowners, published in 1687 but current as of `685, shows, Olof's son John
2 CONC  Stille as the  owner of the land at Moyamensing...  
2 CONT
2 CONT See Van Silley, Stille Notes (1979) and William Douglas Stilley, William Davis Stilley Family History (1978) manuscripts in the collection the National Genealogical Society Libra,,,,The Full name of Olof was Olof Persson Stille, m
2 CONC eaning that he was the son of Per (or Peter) Stille..."
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Records of Ann E. Hunter, Ann E. Hunter  Stille arrived in New Sweden with his family on the "Charitas" in 1641. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Although he had been prominent in New Sweden, he fell out of favor with the governor.
2 CONT
2 CONT  In 1648 Stille purchased a clae calf fro  Rev. John Campanius Holm. After Campanius returned to Sweden that same year, Governor Printz "immediately sent over and caused aforesaid male calf to be taken from him"
2 CONT
2 CONT  Stille was one of the 22 signers of the 1653 complaint against Printz, and was accused by Printz as being one of the three ringleaders in this "munity". 
2 CONT
2 CONT Stille had moved to Moyamensing and the last known mention of his name was when WIlliam Penn reissued the patent to the four Moyamensing landowners, WIlliam (Olof) Stille, Andrew Bankson (Andreas Bengtsson), Lasse Andresws (Lars A
2 CONC ndersson Collins) and John Matson. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Olaf Stille in his later years had been much beloved by the Indians whose name for him as derived from the fact the Olof and a black beard, apparently an oddity among the contemporary Swedes."
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Variants of (Peter's son) Petersson, Pederson, Persson
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Per Stille, father of Olaf, lived on the island of SoIö in Roslagen, northeast of Stockholm. He was a relatively prosperous supervisor of the Penningby estate in Länna parish. By 1627 Per Stille had retired and was granted land by
2 CONC  the owners of Penningby on a nearby island called Humblö. His son, Olaf, traveled to New Sweden with his family. His sister appeared to be in the same group married to Mans Svensson Lom.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olof_Persson_Stille:
2 CONT
2 CONT Olof Persson Stille was a pioneer settler in the Swedish colony of New Sweden. He served as the first chief justice of the Upland Court.
2 CONT
2 CONT Olof Persson Stille was born in Sweden on the island of SoIö in Roslagen, northeast of Stockholm, the son of Per Stille, supervisor of the Penningby estate in Länna parish, Norrtälje Municipality in Uppland, north of Stockholm. In
2 CONC  1627 when Per Stille retired, he was granted land by the owners of Penningby on a nearby island called Humblö in the Stockholm archipelago. This was where Olof Stille married and began his family.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT New Sweden In May 1641, the Charitas departed for New Sweden. The passengers included Olof Stille, his wife, a daughter and son. Also on board were Olof's younger brother Axel Stille and the family of Måns Svensson Lom, whose wife
2 CONC  appears to have been Olof's younger sister.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT In New Sweden, Olof Stille settled at a place located between present Crum Creek and Ridley Creek. The property of Stille was at the mouth of Ridley Creek, now Eddystone, Pennsylvania. Local Indians became acquainted with Olof Sti
2 CONC lle. They considered his heavy, black beard unusual conferring the name Tequirassy on him because of it. Stille became a leader among the settlers and played a key role in promoting the July 1653 list of grievances, which was subm
2 CONC itted to Governor Johan Printz, protesting his strict rule. Printz considered this action mutiny and subsequently returned to Sweden.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Upland Court After the surrender of New Sweden to the Dutch West India Company, Governor Peter Stuyvesant agreed to allow the Swedish colonists living north of the Christina River to govern themselves. The Upland Court at Chester 
2 CONC in Delaware County, Pennsylvania, was organized in 1656. Olof Stille served as its chief justice until his retirement in 1664.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wolley Stille Wolley Stille, a house near Chester which was first completed about 1700, was named for Stille, who owned a neighboring property. Wolley Stille is located in Nether Providence Township, Delaware County, Pennsylvania[
2 CONC 4] The house is now in the National Register of Historic Places. Olof Stille moved to Moyamensing (now south Philadelphia)where he died about 1684.[
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT Olof Stille arrived in New Sweden with his family on the Charitias in 1641. Achieving early prominence in New Sweden, he was called upon by the Governor Printz to deliver a protest to the Dutch in 1646. Soon thereafter he fell out
2 CONC  of favor with the governor. In 1648 Stille purchased a malf calf from Rev. Johan Campanius Holm....
2 CONT
2 CONT Stille not only was one of the 22 signers of the 1653 complaint against Prinz but also, as we have seen, was accused by Printz as being one of the three ringleaders in this "mutiny". After Rising's arrival Stille signed the 1654 o
2 CONC ath of allegiance. Under Dutch rule he served as one of the first magistrates for the "up-river" settlers in present Pennsylvania. Initially the Stille family settled at a place called Tequirassy next to present Ridley Creek, then
2 CONC  called "Olle Stillen's Kill". Governor Printz in 1653 described this settlement as consisting of three plantations with 12 morgans (25 acres) of cultivated land. Under Dutch rule Olof Stille moved to Moyamensing next to WIcaco. H
2 CONC Is intial patent for this land was issued 3 June 1664. The last discovered mention of his name occured 21 July 1684 when WIlliam Penn reissued the patent to the four Moyamensing landowners, William (Olof) Stille, Andrew Bnakson (A
2 CONC ndreas Bengtsson), Lasse Andrews (Lars Andersson Collins) and John Matson. Olof Stille must have died shortly thereafter, as Holme's map of Pennsylvania landowners, published in 1687 but current as of 685, shows, Olof's son John S
2 CONC tille as the owner of the land at Moyamensing... 
2 CONT
2 CONT See Van Silley, Stille Notes (1979) and William Douglas Stilley, William Davis Stilley Family History (1978) manuscripts in the collection the National Genealogical Society Libra,,,,The Full name of Olof was Olof Persson Stille, m
2 CONC eaning that he was the son of Per (or Peter) Stille..."
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Records of Ann E. Hunter, Ann E. Hunter Stille arrived in New Sweden with his family on the "Charitas" in 1641. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Although he had been prominent in New Sweden, he fell out of favor with the governor.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1648 Stille purchased a clae calf fro Rev. John Campanius Holm. After Campanius returned to Sweden that same year, Governor Printz "immediately sent over and caused aforesaid male calf to be taken from him"
2 CONT
2 CONT Stille was one of the 22 signers of the 1653 complaint against Printz, and was accused by Printz as being one of the three ringleaders in this "munity". 
2 CONT
2 CONT Stille had moved to Moyamensing and the last known mention of his name was when WIlliam Penn reissued the patent to the four Moyamensing landowners, WIlliam (Olof) Stille, Andrew Bankson (Andreas Bengtsson), Lasse Andresws (Lars A
2 CONC ndersson Collins) and John Matson. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Olaf Stille in his later years had been much beloved by the Indians whose name for him as derived from the fact the Olof and a black beard, apparently an oddity among the contemporary Swedes."
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.genealogy.com/ftm/w/a/r/James--P-Ward/GENE1-0001.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Wife is Unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://legendsofthefamily.blogspot.com/2014/09/olof-persson-stille-swedish-connection.html
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Source ===
2 CONT @R300960519@  Emigrants to Pennsylvania, 1641-1819   1,48363::0 https://search.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/sse.dll?db=FLHG-EmigrantsPA&h=312118&ti=0&indiv=try&gss=pt  1,48363::312118 Olof Peterson Stille was the son of Peter Stille. he w
2 CONC as born in Penningsby Manor, Ruslagen, Sweden in the year 1610. From two publications, Olof Stille in New Sweden by Fritz Nordstrom, and the Stille Family in America 1641-1772 by Peter Stebbins Craig, we learn much about our Swedi
2 CONC sh forefather, Olof Stille. He was an impetuous youth which led to his expulsion from Sweden. As a young man in the new world, he was a prosperous plantation owner. In his later years, he was admired and respected as a magistrate.
2 CONT
2 CONT On May 3, 1641, Olof Stille, his wife and two children (Ella then 7years old, and Anders, then one-and-a-half years old ) boarded the Charitas in Stockholm for the long voyage to New Sweden, (Wilmington, Delaware), via Goteborg. A
2 CONC fter a stormy voyage, they arrived in November 1641. Stille went on to become a very prosperous plantation owner. He resided on Tinicum Island and owned the land between Ridley and Crum Creeks. In 1658, after the Dutch took posses
2 CONC sion of the country, he was one of the four commissioners or magistrates appointed to administer justice among the inhabitants, neighboring colonists, and the Indians, the later giving him the name, "Black Beard." A bronze Memoria
2 CONC l above the South Broad Street entrance to City Hall in Philadelphia, honors Olof Stille and the early Swedish settlers. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Many of the direct descendants of Olof Stille migrated from the Wilmington/Philadelphia area to New Jersey. Their gravestones may be seen at Head of River Church, Upper Township, New Jersey, and the United Methodist Church in Tuck
2 CONC ahoe, New Jersey. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in Passyunk, Philadelphia in 1684.
