1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Karin Mårtensdotter Dingelvik=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Karin was the owner of all the salvation homes in Steneby at the beginning of the 1560s. She had inherited them from her mother Karin Svensdotter
2 CONT * Karin did not legally marry according to status to her partner Anders Persson. Source: the book Steneby
2 CONT * In the 1563 census, Sundsbyn is listed among Karin Mårtensdotter's estates in Dingelvik. In the 18th century, the farm became part of the large estates that included Baldersnäs and which eventually came to belong to Leonard Magn
2 CONC us Uggla and later his son Carl Fredrik Uggla. In the 19th century, it became a company of Carl Fredrik Waern and Baldersnäs and the farm was used jointly with Baldersnäs. In 1928, the farm was purchased by Karl Oskar Johansson, b
2 CONC orn in 1897 in Starlanda, Råggärd and married to Tilda Bryntesson from Ödskölt. The farm has been used for many years by their son Arvid Johansson from 1922-2010. See "Gamla Steneby" part 2 pages 596 and 838.
2 CONT * Fact 1: Karin was a nurse at all the rescue homes in Steneby in the early 1560s. She had snatched them from her mother Karin Svensdotter. It was said that Karin wasn’t legally married to her partner Anders Persson.
2 CONT * Death: About 1570 in Grangärde, Dalarnas, Sweden
2 CONT
2 CONT =Partner/Married?=
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Anders Persson ''' born around 1469 and died before 1560 who lived at/in Dingelvik, Vedbo. He had a relationship with Karin Mårtensdotter (b around 1472).  We don’t know if they were legally married or not.  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''They had 5 children'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Per Anderson''' (around 1493); 
2 CONT * '''Nils Anderson'''  (around 1495); 
2 CONT * '''Mårten Anderson'''  (around 1497); 
2 CONT * '''Karin Andersdotter'''  (around 1499);
2 CONT * '''Brita Andersdotter'''  (around 1501).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sok.riksarkivet.se/?ValdSortering=DatumStigande&PageSize=100&Sokord=Anders+Svensson&EndastDigitaliserat=False&typAvLista=Standard&AvanceradSok=False&page=75&FacettLimits=g9oV3g%3A0&FacettFilter=register_facet%24Brev%3A&
2 CONC postid=sdhk_37046&tab=post&FacettState=undefined%3Ac%7C#tab '''SDHK no: 37046''']
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Dating: 1511 July 23'''
2 CONT * '''Place of issue: Uppsala''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Issuer: Mårten Jönsson, Anders Jönsson'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Content
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Mårten Jönsson and Anders Jönsson inform Svante Nilsson that they (21/7) were in court for the judgment chapter but were not accused of anything other than that Anders Svensson "shouted" at them during the past men's meeting in
2 CONC  Stockholm, and that when they were again yesterday correct in the presence of Bishop Mats in Strängnäs and others heard bad words, however no worse than that they were in Svante's service. The archbishop absolved them but forbade
2 CONC  them to travel anywhere before his return. A man named Alf has said that he wants to separate them from Svante, which they oppose. Olof narrowly escaped the archbishop's reluctance because on Thursday he promised Svante service a
2 CONC nd received money from him, and the archbishop wanted him for Kristiern Bengtsson. The letter-writers ask Svante to persuade the archbishop to release them, as they have nothing to eat,'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Literature and commentary: Handwritten by Mårten Jönsson (see ASB p. 369). Previously to "Sturearkivet A, Breve til Svante Nielssøn fra flere Afsendere" in the Danish National Archives. About Sturearkivet see essay in Archive, soc
2 CONC iety and research 2004:1-2, pp. 53 ff.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://forum.rotter.se/index.php?topic=65483.10;wap2 Forum Rotter]
2 CONT
2 CONT Gabriel Wallgren : In Valbo hårad's dombok 1622 (Edestam's summaries) you can read the following: Sven Eriksson, an old man of about 100 years old, born in Nordahls district in Torbjörnebyn and now living in Solberga in Högsäter s
2 CONC n in Valbo district, testified that in Gustav I's time he bought a hall. Anders Pedersson at Dyndevik [Dingelvik, Stenby sn, Vedbo hd, Gabriel's note], Säter's farm in Ör sn by 2 tax farmers Lars Katt and Olof Katt [Olof Katt in S
2 CONC äter, Ör sn, landscape deeds 1577 and 1578 according to Pähr Skrede, Gothenburg] for 80 mk örtige, and those and their heirs who sold it shall own it, but clean it up man by man and shift for:te Anders Persson's manger wellb. Nils
2 CONC  Månsson to Broo with hall. well Brun Jöransson and since then it has been under salvation and still is. But he does not know how the same Sæter came to salvation land from treasure.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nils Månsson is identical to the Nils Månsson [Roos af Hjelmsäter] who registered at Bro in Bro sn, Näs härad, Värmland county and who was married to a granddaughter (not the daughter of Anders Persson) [see about this in JE Almqv
2 CONC ist, Herrgårdarna i Sweden during the Reformation period 1523-1611, Stockholm 1960, pp. 334f; cf. aa p 332.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Skrede and I have explained the circumstance that Brun Göransson is mentioned in the dombok notice in 1622 with the fact that his wife was also in some way one of Anders Persson's heirs, possibly married to a sister of Nils Månsso
2 CONC n's wife
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://www.hembygd.se/steneby/plats/312850/text/57689 Great Ocean, by Association: Steneby Hembygdsförening]
2 CONT
2 CONT Stora Havden was the salvation home mentioned in the first land registers in 1535. In the 1560s, Stora Havden, like all other salvation farms in Steneby, was owned by Karin Mårtensdotter from Dingelvik. She was the widow of Anders
2 CONC  Persson and she was the granddaughter of the gunsmith Sven Jonsson in Dingelvik. The farm was farmed by a country dweller and the first named such was called "Mattis i Hafden" who lived here in the 1560s.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the turn of the 19th century, Stora Havden was owned by Brynte Eriksson, one of the "seven hopefuls". He was born in Håbol in 1778. Brynte was married to Maria Lisa Jansdotter, born in 1779, daughter of Jan Segolsson in Gråsjö,
2 CONC  Ärtemark. Their daughter Maria Catharina, married to her cousin Peter Eriksson from Ulfsbyn, became the next owner of Stora Havden. Peter was very musical, which was passed down to later generations. After his death in 1865, Stor
2 CONC a Havden was sold to Saugbrugsforeningen in Fredrikshald. They leased the farm to August Hansson from Taxviken, born in 1845 and married to the daughter of Peter and Maria Catharina, Anna Greta Petersdotter. They had children Melk
2 CONC er, twins Albert and Carl, Bernhard and daughter Maria who died when she was 4 years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1892, the men's building burned down and a new one similar to the old one was then built. August Hansson died in 1913 and his wife in 1920. The son Albert Hansson took over the lease. He lived in Åsen and farmed both farms. In 
2 CONC 1938 he handed over the lease to his son Henrik Hansson. After a fire in the barn in 1945, the Saugbrugsforeningen sold part of the farm to Henrik Hansson. He was married to Hildur née Jakobsson and they have five daughters. Nowad
2 CONC ays, youngest daughter Elisabeth lives in Stora Havden with her family. 
2 CONT
2 CONT See "Old Steneby" part 2 pages 351 and 763.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sv.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dingelvik Dingelvik, by Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT Dingelvik is a place in Bengtsfors municipality , Västra Götaland county , located in Steneby parish . The development was defined by Statistics Norway as a small town in 1990 and was deregistered as such in 2020 due to the fact t
2 CONC hat the number of residents was below 50.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Dingelvik Farm'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The farm in Dingelvik has been known since the Middle Ages, and the squire Sven Jonsson (half lily) (died around 1484), signed himself to Dingelvik . The farm was inherited within the Halsten Petersson family , and passed to the o
2 CONC lder Forstenas family (ram's head), and after a division into Norra and Södra Dingelvik, during the 17th century, northern Dingelvik was owned by the Svinhufvud family , while southern Dingelvik was owned by the Ahlefeldt family .
2 CONT
2 CONT A man building on Södra Dingelvik was built around 1848-1850 by the then owner Baron Henry de Reverony St. Cyr.
2 CONT
2 CONT The farm is today owned by the Kling family.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sv.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halsten_Peterssons_%C3%A4tt Halsten Petersson's Family, by Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Halsten Petersson's family ,''' also called half lily , is a contemporary conventional name for a Swedish medieval squire family that took its name from the progenitor, knight and bailiff Halsten Petersson. The family died out 
2 CONC before Sweden's Riddarhus was founded in 1625, which is why it was never introduced there as nobility . The members of the family wore a red scotch, with half a golden lily laid in beams in the upper field.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''History'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Two land exchanges from 1377 and 1380 give good insight into the mutual kinship of several members.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family Tree'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Halsten Petersson, knight (1355),''' landowner and bailiff. His origin is unknown, but a (half) brother is mentioned, Arvid Petersson, who carried a spar in the coat of arms. 1336 and 1338 goods as thanks for faithful service
2 CONC . he was given by King Magnus Eriksson but participated in King Erik Magnusson 's rebellion against his father, and is mentioned among Erik's five pledges in 1357, but is stated to have died on March 1, 1358. [ 3 ] '''Married Märt
2 CONC a (Margareta) Knutsdotter ( Tre Rosor till Mörby ), daughter to Knut (Tre Rosor till Mörby) and Kristina Bengtsdotter in Haneström, daughter of the lawman in Västergötland Bengt Hafridsson .''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1- '''Johan Halstensson'''
2 CONT >>>* 1- '''Halsten Johansson.''' According to Peder Månsson Utter (1566-1623) in ( Collectanea genealogica ), Halsten Johansson carried a fragmented shield with the upper half of a split (not split) lily gilt in the upper field. A
2 CONC ccording to the same source, Halsten was the father of Håkan Halstensson. 
2 CONT >>>>>* 1- '''Håkan Halstensson,''' knight, named 1346-1380. [ 8 ] Married to Bengta, daughter of the knight and councilor Sigge Magnusson ( Vinstorpaätten , Håkan Tunesson's family ) and Ramborg. 
2 CONT >>>>>>>>* 1 '''NN Håkansdotter,''' married to Ulf Bengtsson ( Sparre of Hjulsta and Ängsö ), parents of, among others, Bishop Sigge in Strängnäs.
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>* 2 - '''Sven Jonsson (half lily) til Dinglevik,''' Steneby, died about 1484, was a gunsmith and signed up to Dingelvik , which was a salvation farm , later a manor in ( Steneby parish ), and married on 5 October 1449 [ 11 ] at
2 CONC  Stola with an Ingeborg Bytesdotter, the daughter of Tore Byting . 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>'''Three children are known:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>* '''Ragnhild Svensdotter (half lily)
2 CONT >>>>* '''Karin Svensdotter (half lily)''' to Dinglevik, Steneby.'''
2 CONT >>>>* '''Botilla Svensdotter (half lily)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * 2 - '''Thord Halstensson'''
2 CONT * 3 - '''Knut Halstensson (half lily)''' was married to Katarina Guttormsdotter, daughter of Guttorm Toresson (split      shield) and Inga Gregersdotter ( Malstaätten )
2 CONT * 4 - '''Håkan Hallstensson''' During the civil war in 1364, had his estates confiscated by King Håkan Magnusson , which Håkan gave to Nils Bengtsson (Lion-faced, Bengt Nilsson's family)
